# Template. SITE_DOMAIN comes from deploy/glasapp.env; glas.example.com is a placeholder.
# Caddy gets and renews the HTTPS certificate on its own: ports 80 and 443 must reach this
# host, and the domain's DNS record must point straight at it (no CDN proxy in between).
#
# There is no access log: the site block has no `log` directive, on purpose. The app writes its own
# request log with the path only (server/utils/httpLogger.ts).
#
# Caddy's ERROR log is separate. Whenever the app is not answering (a 502 during `docker compose up
# -d --build`) it writes the request: the full URI, EVERY header except Cookie and Authorization, and
# the visitor's IP. A password-reset link carries its one-time token in the query, and the job calls
# (cron, admin) send their secret in X-Admin-Secret / X-Cron-Secret, so unfiltered both would sit in
# the container log. Measured with caddy:2: unfiltered, the token, the user agent, the IP and an
# X-Admin-Secret header all appeared; with this filter the line keeps the path and method and nothing
# else about the caller. To re-check after a Caddy upgrade: stop the app container, request
# https://<domain>/x?token_hash=SECRET-T with the header "X-Admin-Secret: SECRET-S", then
# `docker compose logs caddy` must show neither SECRET-T nor SECRET-S.
{
	log {
		format filter {
			wrap json
			request>uri regexp \?.* ""
			request>headers delete
			request>remote_ip delete
			request>client_ip delete
			request>remote_port delete
		}
	}
}

{$SITE_DOMAIN:glas.example.com} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy app:5000
}
